ENG-10  WineMetalGL gets the window's own metal view: it follows the window and the title bar stays opaque.

dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal.c (winemac.so). Lullaby, 2026-10-27. LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine.

What: the table `macdrv_functions` the engine exports is shaped for D3DMetal (hwnd, cocoa_window, cocoa_view,
client_cocoa_view, ...). WineMetalGL, built against stock Wine 11 (hwnd, cocoa_window, client_view), asks for
`get_win_data` and reads the client view from the third field, which was empty. It then gave up winemac's metal view
and put a view of its own into the window, placed from the window's geometry: the picture did not follow a window that
was moved, black areas appeared, and, as that view never goes through windowDidDrawContent, the window stayed see-through
(a transparent title bar). Now a caller from winemetalgl.so (dladdr on the return address) gets the client view in the
third field too; any other caller, D3DMetal included, gets the data exactly as before.
Requires: WineMetalGL 3.x (CMP-10); nothing for other renderers.
Other bases: unchanged table in WineForge/Whisky-style builds is not known; the patch touches only Dormison's d3dmetal.c.
Test: Isaac (32-bit, OpenGL) under WineMetalGL: WineMetalGL's log (WINEMETALGL_LOG=trace) says "winemac metal view"
instead of "own view in the Cocoa window" for each window.
Verified on: Dormison r7, built in scratch/local-bridge/dormison-r7 (2026-10-27); WineMetalGL's trace line checked there; moving and resizing by the owner (see ledger).
Switch: none.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal.c b/dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal.c
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal.c
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal.c
@@ -121,10 +121,23 @@
 static void my_macdrv_init_display_devices(BOOL p1)
 {
     TRACE("%d - no-op\n", p1);
+}
+
+/* WineMetalGL (a renderer built against stock Wine 11, struct macdrv_win_data { hwnd, cocoa_window, client_view })
+ * asks for this table too and reads the client view from the third field, where D3DMetal's older layout holds
+ * cocoa_view. Left empty there, WineMetalGL gives up the window's own metal view and places a view of its own, which
+ * neither follows the window when it moves nor goes through windowDidDrawContent (a see-through title bar). Only a
+ * caller from winemetalgl.so gets the client view in that field; D3DMetal sees what it always saw. */
+static BOOL caller_is_winemetalgl(void *address)
+{
+    Dl_info info;
+
+    return dladdr(address, &info) && info.dli_fname && strstr(info.dli_fname, "winemetalgl");
 }
 
 static struct d3dmetal_macdrv_win_data *my_get_win_data(HWND hwnd)
 {
+    void *caller = __builtin_return_address(0);
     struct macdrv_win_data *data;
     struct d3dmetal_macdrv_win_data *d3dm_data;
     struct macdrv_client_surface *surface;
@@ -163,6 +176,7 @@
     d3dm_data->hwnd = data->hwnd;
     d3dm_data->cocoa_window = data->cocoa_window;
     d3dm_data->client_cocoa_view = surface->cocoa_view;
+    if (caller_is_winemetalgl(caller)) d3dm_data->cocoa_view = surface->cocoa_view;
     d3dm_data->window_rect = data->rects.window;
     d3dm_data->whole_rect = data->rects.visible;
     d3dm_data->client_rect = data->rects.client;
